{"hash":"d3af53b1b6f64526","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":98.243,"said":"Here, north of the temple, is the Gyanvapi, the well of wisdom, within its white marble enclosure of carved jali screens, its mouth covered over and heaped with flowers. The Kashi Khanda tells that Shiva himself dug it with his trident to bathe the linga, and that its water is knowledge itself. The great stone Nandi beside it was a gift, it is recorded, from the royal house of Nepal in the nineteenth century, and notice which way he faces: not toward today’s temple, but north, toward where the older temple stood. The history of this place is told through many hands. The temple of Vishweshwara was destroyed and rebuilt more than once over the centuries. In 1585 Raja Todar Mal, a minister at the Mughal court, helped raise it again. In 1669, on the orders of the Mughal emperor Aurangzeb, that temple was demolished, and the Gyanvapi mosque was built on its site; it stands beyond this court today. Tradition says that when the temple fell, a priest leapt into this well with the linga to keep it safe. And the pilgrims never stopped coming. In 1828 Baiza Bai, widow of the Scindia ruler of Gwalior, built a pillared hall here, over the well, for them. Now turn to the temple itself. It was a queen from Indore who gave Vishwanath a home again.\n\nDid you know? Baiza Bai’s colonnade had more than forty pillars; sources differ on the date of the Nepali Nandi, from the early 1800s to about 1860.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1251},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1267,"end":1401}],"words":[[0.098,0.421,0,4],[0.723,0.325,6,11],[1.06,0.096,12,14],[1.168,0.108,15,18],[1.288,0.517,19,25],[2.081,0.132,27,29],[2.225,0.096,30,33],[2.334,0.817,34,42],[3.403,0.156,44,47],[3.572,0.216,48,52],[3.8,0.156,53,55],[3.968,0.505,56,62],[4.75,0.252,64,70],[5.014,0.192,71,74],[5.218,0.349,75,80],[5.579,0.409,81,87],[6.0,0.649,88,97],[6.661,0.156,98,100],[6.829,0.373,101,107],[7.213,0.325,108,1